---
import BlockLayout from '../layouts/BlockLayout.astro';
import atlas from '../data/weather-atlas.json';
import '../styles/weather-atlas.css';
import {radiusBoundary} from '../scripts/weather-atlas.mjs';
const project=(lat:number,lon:number)=>({x:400+(lon-atlas.center.lon)*Math.cos(atlas.center.lat*Math.PI/180)*69.0934/25*285,y:360-(lat-atlas.center.lat)*69.0934/25*285});
const pts=(arr:number[][])=>arr.map(([lat,lon])=>{const p=project(lat,lon);return `${p.x},${p.y}`;}).join(' ');
const boundary=pts(radiusBoundary(atlas.center,25));
const years=Array.from({length:35},(_,i)=>({year:1991+i,lax:atlas.observedCalendarYears.find(o=>o.station==='lax'&&o.year===1991+i),downtown:atlas.observedCalendarYears.find(o=>o.station==='downtown'&&o.year===1991+i)}));
const s=atlas.seasons[2],z=atlas.zones[0];
const sources=atlas.sources as {id:string,title:string,url:string,use:string}[];
const stations=atlas.stations as any[];
const tPoints=(v:number[])=>v.map((t,i)=>`${80+i*56},${290-(t-40)*4}`).join(' ');
const methods=atlas.mapping as any[];
const history=atlas.historical as any[];
---
<BlockLayout title="A year within reach · El Segundo Weather & Living Atlas" description="A 25-mile historical climate atlas around El Segundo. NOAA 1991–2020 station normals, seasonal routines, an illustrative microclimate map and useful object concepts." image="/images/weather-atlas/social.png" isolated immersive showNetworkStrip={false} themeColor="#f4f0e7" jsonLd={{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'CreativeWork',name:atlas.title,url:'https://pointcast.xyz/weather-atlas/',datePublished:atlas.date,description:'Independent historical climate research and illustrative seasonal living guidance; not a forecast.',author:{'@type':'Organization',name:'Codex for PointCast'},isAccessibleForFree:true}} alternates={[{type:'application/json',href:'/weather-atlas.json',title:'Atlas data and source methods'}]}>
<div class="wa" data-atlas>
<header class="wa-nav"><a href="/">POINTCAST ↗</a><a href="/object-library/">OBJECT LIBRARY ↗</a><a href="#sources">SOURCES ↓</a></header>
<section class="wa-hero" aria-labelledby="atlas-title"><div><p class="wa-kicker">EL SEGUNDO / 25 MILES / A LIVING ATLAS</p><h1 id="atlas-title">A year<br/><em>within reach.</em></h1><p class="wa-dek">The ocean changes the afternoon.<br/>The hills change the wind.<br/>A few good objects change the day.</p><p>Read the past. Make a seasonal plan. Keep space for what the weather does next.</p><a class="wa-button" href="#living">Find your seasonal rhythm ↓</a></div><figure><img src="/images/weather-atlas/cover.svg" width="960" height="860" alt="Original square-glasses guides: a marine cloud, a sun, a rain shell and an ember, arranged around a 25-mile circle" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>FOUR ORIGINAL GUIDES / ONE SHARED YEAR</figcaption></figure></section>
<aside class="wa-note"><strong>HISTORICAL CLIMATE + PLANNING IDEAS</strong><p>1991–2020 normals, dated station history and qualitative microclimates. This is an editorial atlas, not a live forecast, air-quality map or promise of comfort. No real objects are available to borrow.</p></aside>
<section class="wa-section" id="living" aria-labelledby="living-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">01 / THE SEASONAL SUITABILITY MAP</p><h2 id="living-title">Choose a rhythm.<br/><em>Then check the day.</em></h2><p>“Best” depends on your body, route, home and purpose. These are useful starting choices, not a ranked neighborhood recommendation. Map shapes are illustrative; station dots mark evidence.</p></div>
<div class="wa-controls"><fieldset><legend>Season</legend><div>{atlas.seasons.map(season=><button type="button" data-season={season.id} aria-pressed={season.id==='summer'}>{season.name}<small>{season.months}</small></button>)}</div></fieldset><fieldset><legend>What matters today?</legend><div><button type="button" data-purpose="outdoor" aria-pressed="true">Outdoor time</button><button type="button" data-purpose="comfort" aria-pressed="false">Shade & comfort</button><button type="button" data-purpose="home" aria-pressed="false">Ventilation</button><button type="button" data-purpose="water" aria-pressed="false">Water & gear</button></div></fieldset></div>
<div class="wa-map-layout"><div><figure class="wa-map"><svg viewBox="0 0 800 720" aria-labelledby="map-title map-desc"><title id="map-title">Illustrative microclimates inside a 25-mile radius</title><desc id="map-desc">El Segundo is the center. Ocean lies west, inland basin northeast, South Bay southeast, hills south and north, Burbank near the northern edge. Use the labeled buttons below for equivalent zone selection. Shapes are schematic, not measured contours.</desc><defs><clipPath id="radius-clip"><polygon points={boundary}/></clipPath><pattern id="terrain" width="18" height="18" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M0 18L18 0" stroke="#ffffff" opacity=".3"/></pattern></defs><circle cx="400" cy="360" r="286" fill="#b4c9d0"/><g clip-path="url(#radius-clip)"><polygon points={pts([[34.4,-118.65],[34.05,-118.55],[34.01,-118.49],[33.95,-118.45],[33.87,-118.43],[33.80,-118.40],[33.75,-118.41],[33.70,-118.34],[33.71,-118.29],[33.76,-118.25],[33.76,-118.1],[33.4,-118],[34.4,-118]])} fill="#ddd4b9"/>
<g data-map-zone="coast" class="selected" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Select ocean edge microclimate"><polygon points={pts([[34.06,-118.53],[34.02,-118.49],[33.95,-118.45],[33.85,-118.43],[33.81,-118.40],[33.82,-118.35],[33.94,-118.36],[34.04,-118.43]])} fill="#88b4b6"/><title>Ocean edge</title></g>
<g data-map-zone="basin" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Select inland basin microclimate"><polygon points={pts([[34.04,-118.43],[33.94,-118.36],[33.90,-118.22],[34.00,-118.02],[34.16,-118.07],[34.16,-118.4]])} fill="#ddb079"/><title>Inland basin</title></g>
<g data-map-zone="south" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Select South Bay and port plain microclimate"><polygon points={pts([[33.94,-118.36],[33.90,-118.22],[34.0,-118.02],[33.76,-118.1],[33.76,-118.25],[33.79,-118.30],[33.82,-118.35]])} fill="#b6bb95"/><title>South Bay and port plain</title></g>
<g data-map-zone="hills" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Select hills and exposed slopes microclimate"><polygon points={pts([[33.81,-118.4],[33.82,-118.35],[33.79,-118.3],[33.71,-118.29],[33.70,-118.34],[33.75,-118.41]])} fill="#8d9c76"/><polygon points={pts([[34.05,-118.55],[34.10,-118.62],[34.22,-118.6],[34.18,-118.4],[34.16,-118.4],[34.04,-118.43]])} fill="#8d9c76"/><title>Hills and exposed slopes: qualitative terrain class, sparse observations</title></g>
<g data-map-zone="north" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Select northern inland edge microclimate"><polygon points={pts([[34.16,-118.4],[34.18,-118.4],[34.28,-118.5],[34.34,-118.28],[34.16,-118.07]])} fill="#d99772"/><title>Northern inland edge</title></g>
</g><polygon points={boundary} fill="none" stroke="#263c42" stroke-dasharray="7 7"/><text x="154" y="353" class="wa-map-ocean">PACIFIC</text><text x="154" y="375" class="wa-map-ocean">OCEAN</text><text x="400" y="56" text-anchor="middle">N ↑</text><text x="400" y="676" text-anchor="middle">25 MILES · STRAIGHT-LINE RADIUS</text>
{stations.map(st=>{const p=project(st.lat,st.lon);return <g><circle cx={p.x} cy={p.y} r="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#152c36" stroke-width="3"/><text x={p.x+10} y={p.y-10}>{st.short}</text></g>})}<circle cx="400" cy="360" r="6" fill="#cf6347"/><text x="295" y="387" font-weight="700">EL SEGUNDO</text><path d="M165 615h114m-114-5v10m114-10v10" stroke="#152c36"/><text x="170" y="600">10 mi (local scale)</text></svg><figcaption>Radius sampled with a great-circle formula. Coastline and zone boundaries are schematic. No gridded weather values were estimated between station dots. <a href="#methods">Read the method ↓</a></figcaption></figure>
<div class="wa-zones" aria-label="Microclimate selection">{atlas.zones.map(zone=><button type="button" data-zone={zone.id} aria-pressed={zone.id==='coast'}><span style={`background:${zone.color}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{zone.name}</button>)}</div><p class="wa-small">All named examples are inside the chosen circle except where a larger regional terrain is explicitly clipped to the circle. This is a distance boundary, not driving distance, a municipal boundary or a service area.</p></div>
<article class="wa-plan"><p class="wa-kicker" data-live="season">{s.name} · {s.months}</p><h3 data-live="title">{s.title}</h3><p data-live="summary">{s.summary}</p><hr/><h4 data-live="zone">{z.name}</h4><p class="wa-small" data-live="places">{z.places}</p><p data-live="pattern">{z.pattern}</p><div class="wa-recommend"><p class="wa-kicker">YOUR STARTING PLAN</p><p data-live="season-advice">{s.outdoor}</p><p data-live="zone-advice">{z.advice.outdoor}</p></div><p class="wa-small" data-live="evidence">{z.evidence}</p><h4>A useful object kit</h4><ul data-kit><li><a href="/object-library/#brim">BRIM · shade reminder</a></li><li><a href="/object-library/#pane">PANE · window cue</a></li><li><a href="/object-library/#turn">TURN · focus / pause</a></li></ul><p class="wa-small">Design concepts only. No object here is safety equipment, a calibrated sensor or a forecast device.</p><a class="wa-button" href="/object-library/">Rehearse a library visit ↗</a></article></div><p class="wa-sr" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-live="status">Summer, ocean edge, outdoor time.</p><noscript><p>The default summer/coast plan and all research remain readable. Enable JavaScript to switch the map's planning cards.</p></noscript>
</section>
<section class="wa-section wa-normals" id="normals" aria-labelledby="normal-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">02 / THE REPEATING SHAPE · NOAA 1991–2020</p><h2 id="normal-title">The coast keeps<br/><em>a different clock.</em></h2><p>Normals are 30-year reference statistics, not the most common day, a forecast or evidence that every year is the same. Four stations reveal contrast; they leave gaps across hills and neighborhoods. Source quality flags may include estimated months.</p></div>
<div class="wa-charts"><figure><h3>Normal daily maximum temperature · °F</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 800 350" role="img" aria-labelledby="temperature-title"><title id="temperature-title">Four station monthly normal high-temperature curves. Full values appear in the table below.</title>{[50,60,70,80,90].map(v=><g><path d={`M70 ${290-(v-40)*4}H730`} stroke="#d3cfc4"/><text x="30" y={294-(v-40)*4}>{v}°</text></g>)}{stations.map((st,i)=><polyline points={tPoints(st.high)} fill="none" stroke={st.color} stroke-width={i===0?5:3}/>)}{['J','F','M','A','M','J','J','A','S','O','N','D'].map((m,i)=><text x={80+i*56} y="320" text-anchor="middle">{m}</text>)}</svg><figcaption>{stations.map(st=><span><i style={`background:${st.color}`}></i>{st.short}</span>)}</figcaption></figure><figure><h3>LAX normal precipitation · inches / month</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 800 350" role="img" aria-labelledby="rain-title"><title id="rain-title">LAX rainfall concentrates in the cool season. Full monthly values appear below.</title>{[0,1,2,3,4].map(v=><g><path d={`M70 ${290-v*60}H730`} stroke="#d3cfc4"/><text x="36" y={294-v*60}>{v}</text></g>)}{(stations[0]?.rain||[]).map((v:number,i:number)=><rect x={65+i*56} y={290-v*60} width="30" height={Math.max(1,v*60)} fill="#5b8797"/>)}{['J','F','M','A','M','J','J','A','S','O','N','D'].map((m,i)=><text x={80+i*56} y="320" text-anchor="middle">{m}</text>)}</svg><figcaption>Rain is episodic. An annual sum hides dry stretches, intense storms and year-to-year variation.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="wa-table-wrap"><table><caption>Monthly station normals · °F and inches · 1991–2020. Values transcribed from NOAA CSVs; not live readings.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th>{stations.map(st=><th scope="col">{st.short}<br/>high °F</th>)}<th scope="col">LAX<br/>low °F</th><th scope="col">LAX<br/>rain in</th><th scope="col">Downtown<br/>rain in</th></tr></thead><tbody>{['Jan','Feb','Mar','Apr','May','Jun','Jul','Aug','Sep','Oct','Nov','Dec'].map((m,i)=><tr><th scope="row">{m}</th>{stations.map(st=><td>{st.high[i].toFixed(1)}</td>)}<td>{stations[0]?.low[i].toFixed(1)}</td><td>{stations[0]?.rain[i].toFixed(2)}</td><td>{stations[1]?.rain[i].toFixed(2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<div class="wa-station-grid">{stations.map(st=><article><p class="wa-kicker">{st.id}</p><h3>{st.name}</h3><p>{st.history}</p><p class="wa-small">{st.lat}°N / {Math.abs(st.lon)}°W · {st.elevation} m · {st.distanceMiles.toFixed(1)} mi from center</p><p class="wa-small">{st.quality}</p><a href={st.url}>NOAA monthly source CSV ↗</a></article>)}</div><p class="wa-small">Downtown is a station series with location changes, including Civic Center → USC in 1999 → Frank Hotchkin Memorial Training Center in 2024. The 1991–2020 normal spans earlier sites; the map uses an approximate USC anchor for orientation, not the current 2024 site. The NOAA monthly CSV retains older location metadata. <a href="#sources">Station-history evidence below.</a></p></section>
<section class="wa-section" aria-labelledby="past-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">03 / THE PAST IS NOT ONE WEATHER YEAR</p><h2 id="past-title">A normal.<br/><em>An exception.<br/>A changing baseline.</em></h2><p>Keep four kinds of evidence apart. An observed storm does not establish a trend. A statewide trend does not resolve a street. A future scenario is conditional on assumptions.</p></div><div class="wa-history">{history.map(event=><article><p class="wa-kicker">{event.kind} / {event.period}</p><h3>{event.title}</h3><p>{event.text}</p><a href={event.url}>{event.source} ↗</a></article>)}</div></section>
<section class="wa-section" aria-labelledby="years-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">OBSERVED HISTORY / 35 COMPLETE CALENDAR YEARS</p><h2 id="years-title">The same place.<br/><em>Different years.</em></h2><p>NOAA GHCN-Daily accepted precipitation values, aggregated January–December, 1991–2025. These are observations, separate from the climate normal. No recurrence probability is implied.</p></div><figure class="wa-years-chart"><h3>LAX observed calendar-year rainfall · inches</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 860 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="year-chart-title"><title id="year-chart-title">LAX annual observed rainfall, 1991 to 2025, varies substantially. Full values and coverage are in the expandable table below.</title>{[0,10,20,30].map(v=><g><path d={`M60 ${290-v*8}H825`} stroke="#c5c4b9"/><text x="20" y={294-v*8}>{v}</text></g>)}{years.map((y,i)=><g><rect x={65+i*21.5} y={290-y.lax.precipIn*8} width="15" height={y.lax.precipIn*8} fill={[1998,2013,2023].includes(y.year)?'#cf6b47':'#64899b'}/>{[1991,1998,2005,2013,2020,2025].includes(y.year)&&<text x={72+i*21.5} y="315" text-anchor="middle">{y.year}</text>}</g>)}<path d={`M60 ${290-12.23*8}H825`} stroke="#233c43" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/><text x="625" y={280-12.23*8}>12.23in normal annual sum</text></svg><figcaption>All 365 or 366 calendar days had accepted precipitation values for each displayed year. Trace measured rain is retained as zero. Source records can be revised. Converted daily sums are shown to one decimal; small differences from official annual reports can reflect rounding.</figcaption></figure><details class="wa-years-details"><summary>Read the 35-year observation table and heat context ↓</summary><div class="wa-table-wrap"><table><caption>NOAA raw daily aggregation · “90°F days” counts accepted daily maximums rounded to whole Fahrenheit before the ≥90°F threshold. Station exposure and downtown site moves limit comparisons.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">LAX rain in</th><th scope="col">Downtown rain in</th><th scope="col">LAX 90°F days</th><th scope="col">Downtown 90°F days</th><th scope="col">LAX temp days</th><th scope="col">Downtown temp days</th></tr></thead><tbody>{years.map(y=><tr><th scope="row">{y.year}</th><td>{y.lax.precipIn.toFixed(1)}</td><td>{y.downtown.precipIn.toFixed(1)}</td><td>{y.lax.daysRoundedHighAtLeast90F ?? "—"}</td><td>{y.downtown.daysRoundedHighAtLeast90F ?? "—"}</td><td>{y.lax.tmaxAcceptedDays}/{y.lax.expectedDays}</td><td>{y.downtown.tmaxAcceptedDays}/{y.downtown.expectedDays}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details><p class="wa-small">An em dash means incomplete temperature coverage, not zero hot days. Blank NOAA quality flags and nonmissing values only. Downtown’s 2024 record spans its USC → FHMC move; these raw counts are not a homogenized trend. NOAA traces do not become positive rain amounts. <a href="/weather-atlas/observed-years.csv" download>Download complete observations and coverage CSV ↓</a></p><div class="wa-method-note"><h3>A change in normals is a different question</h3><p>NOAA’s consistent-version comparison gives LAX’s August normal daily high as 75.7°F for recomputed 1981–2010, versus 76.7°F for 1991–2020: +1.0°F. Downtown’s corresponding values are 82.8°F and 84.0°F: +1.2°F. Those overlapping windows share 20 years; this is a baseline comparison, not an annual warming rate or an attribution study.</p><p class="wa-small">Use NOAA’s recomputed comparison files, rather than subtracting two separately released editions with different homogenization. Full comparison rows and source provenance are in <a href="/weather-atlas/evidence.json">the evidence download</a>.</p></div></section>
<section class="wa-section" id="calendar" aria-labelledby="calendar-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">04 / THE ANNUAL LIVING CALENDAR</p><h2 id="calendar-title">Less fighting the year.<br/><em>More moving with it.</em></h2><p>A repeating routine with room for exceptions. These are planning ideas derived from the climate shape; use current warnings, local rules and personal needs to decide the day.</p></div><div class="wa-calendar">{atlas.seasons.map(season=><article style={`--tile:${season.color}`}><p class="wa-kicker">{season.months} / {season.guide}</p><h3>{season.name}</h3><p>{season.summary}</p><dl><dt>Outdoor time</dt><dd>{season.outdoor}</dd><dt>Comfort & shade</dt><dd>{season.comfort}</dd><dt>Home & ventilation</dt><dd>{season.home}</dd><dt>Water & gear</dt><dd>{season.water}</dd></dl><div>{season.objects.map(id=><a href={`/object-library/#${id}`}>{id.toUpperCase()} ↗</a>)}</div></article>)}</div><aside class="wa-official"><h3>Before choosing today</h3><p><a href="https://www.weather.gov/lox/">NWS Los Angeles / Oxnard forecasts and alerts</a> · <a href="https://www.airnow.gov/">AirNow current air quality</a> · <a href="https://www.weather.gov/safety/heat">NWS heat guidance</a> · <a href="https://www.airnow.gov/wildfires/when-smoke-is-in-the-air/">AirNow smoke guidance</a> · <a href="https://water.ca.gov/water-basics/drought">California drought information</a></p><p class="wa-small">This page does not fetch or display current conditions. An object cue cannot replace an alert, smoke monitor or cooling plan.</p></aside></section>
<section class="wa-section" id="methods" aria-labelledby="method-title"><div class="wa-heading"><p class="wa-kicker">05 / HOW PEOPLE MIGHT HAVE MAPPED IT</p><h2 id="method-title">From a station notebook<br/><em>to a layered map.</em></h2><p>Historical methods often answer different questions than a modern comfort map. A useful map carries its evidence and its missing pieces.</p></div><div class="wa-methods">{methods.map((m,i)=><article><span>0{i+1}</span><h3>{m.title}</h3><p>{m.text}</p><p class="wa-small">{m.limit}</p><a href={m.url}>{m.source} ↗</a></article>)}</div><div class="wa-method-note"><h3>What this edition actually does</h3><p>{atlas.method}</p><p>It compares four station normals and builds a qualitative coast / basin / South Bay / hills / northern-edge model. Colors describe terrain and marine influence, not measured temperature. No pixel receives an interpolated value; advice changes by season and purpose. A later quantitative layer would need station QC, elevation and ocean exposure covariates, cross-validation and uncertainty intervals.</p><p>We did not calculate a local warming trend, attribution result, future temperature forecast, rain probability or optimized route. We did not survey shade, individual homes, trail conditions or air pollution. Coastal and hill coverage is sparse; microclimates can shift with wind and marine-layer depth.</p></div></section>
<section class="wa-section wa-downloads" aria-labelledby="download-title"><div><p class="wa-kicker">06 / KEEP THE FIELD NOTES</p><h2 id="download-title">A map is a question<br/><em>you can carry.</em></h2><p>Revisit each season. Notice what your block does. Bring the observation back to a future object prototype.</p></div><div><a class="wa-button" href="/weather-atlas/methodology.md" download>Research + product briefs ↓</a><a class="wa-button" href="/weather-atlas/research-kit.zip" download>Complete reproducible research kit · ZIP ↓</a><a class="wa-button" href="/weather-atlas/normals.csv" download>Monthly normals · CSV ↓</a><a class="wa-button" href="/images/weather-atlas/field-map.svg" download>Illustrative field map · SVG ↓</a><a class="wa-button" href="/weather-atlas.json">Machine edition ↗</a><p class="wa-small">Downloads include source periods and caveats. Original illustrations reference the open Nouns visual culture, with no imported token art and no official affiliation.</p></div></section>
<section class="wa-section wa-sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><div><p class="wa-kicker">07 / SOURCE APPENDIX</p><h2 id="sources-title">Useful things<br/><em>need an honest trail.</em></h2><p>Research accessed 2 October 2026. Government sources and original station files anchor the numeric claims; editorial advice is labeled as inference.</p></div><ol>{sources.map(source=><li id={source.id}><a href={source.url}>{source.title} ↗</a><p>{source.use}</p></li>)}</ol></section>
<footer class="wa-footer"><p>POINTCAST / A YEAR WITHIN REACH<br/><span>RESEARCH & DESIGN BY CODEX · 02 OCT 2026</span></p><nav aria-label="Atlas footer"><a href="/object-library/">Object Library ↗</a><a href="/ues/radius">RADIUS field instrument ↗</a><a href="/">Back to PointCast ↗</a></nav></footer>
</div>
<script>import atlas from '../data/weather-atlas.json';import {initAtlas} from '../scripts/weather-atlas.mjs';const setup=()=>initAtlas(document.querySelector('[data-atlas]'),atlas);setup();document.addEventListener('astro:page-load',setup);</script>
</BlockLayout>
